# Security Review Report — Rule-Based Baseline Script (ATT Project)

**File reviewed:** `baseline.py` (rule-based baseline + weekly feature
engineering, Data Analyst track, Step 5)
**Reviewer:** Cybersecurity Team Member — ATT Project
**Date:** 21 September 2026
**Review type:** Static code review (this script runs offline, not as a
network-facing API, so verification was done by reading the code itself
rather than sending live requests).

---

## 1. Purpose and Context

This script reads raw enrollment and attendance data and produces a
weekly features-and-flags table used both as a rule-based baseline and
as the training input for the logistic regression risk model
(`train_weekly_risk_model.py`). Its output file directly carries real
student attendance data, so weaknesses in how that output is produced or
stored are relevant to the same student-data protection requirement
(ATT-FR-01) that applies to the rest of the ATT system.

---

## 2. Confirmed Findings

### Finding 1 — Student Data Saved in Plain, Unprotected Form
**Code:**
```python
features_clean.to_excel(writer, sheet_name="WeeklyFeaturesAndFlags", index=False)
```
**Description:** The output file stores `student_id` in plain, readable
form for every row, with no anonymization, hashing, or encryption applied
anywhere in the script.

**How this was verified:** Read this line and everything preceding it,
then searched the full script for any of `encrypt`, `hash`, or
`anonymize` — none appear anywhere in the file. This confirms no data
protection step exists between reading the raw data and writing the
final file.

**Relevance to the project:** This is the same file consumed later by
the risk-prediction model, so any exposure here carries real student
attendance data outside of any access-control layer the rest of the
system enforces.

---

### Finding 2 — No Input Validation on Source Data
**Code:**
```python
enrollment = pd.read_excel(IN_PATH, sheet_name="Enrollment")
sessions = pd.read_excel(IN_PATH, sheet_name="AttendanceSessions")
events = pd.read_excel(IN_PATH, sheet_name="AttendanceEvents")
```
**Description:** The script never checks that the expected sheets or
columns (`status`, `week_number`, `session_id`, etc.) exist or contain
valid values before using them.

**How this was verified:** Read the script from top to bottom and
searched for `try` or `except` — neither appears anywhere in the file.
This confirms there is no error handling or validation at any point in
the pipeline.

**Relevance to the project:** If the source file is malformed,
incomplete, or has unexpected values, the script will either crash
without a clear explanation or silently produce incorrect features and
flags that later feed into the risk model.

---

### Finding 3 — No Logging or Audit Trail for Script Execution
**Description:** There is no persistent record of who ran the script,
when, or against which version of the input data.

**How this was verified:** Searched the full script for `logging` or
`log` — found only `print()` statements used for console output during
execution. `print()` output disappears once the terminal session ends,
so there is no durable record of any run.

**Relevance to the project:** If a student is later found to have been
incorrectly flagged (or missed) by the risk system, there is currently
no record connecting a specific script run to a specific data snapshot
to investigate what happened.

---

### Finding 4 — Hardcoded, Windows-Specific File Path
**Code:**
```python
IN_PATH = "data\\processed\\ATT_demo_dataset_converted.xlsx"
```
**Description:** The input path is hardcoded using Windows-style
backslashes.

**How this was verified:** Directly visible in the source line itself —
`\\` is a Windows-style path separator, as opposed to `/` used on
Linux/Mac. No further testing was needed to confirm this; it is stated
literally in the code.

**Relevance to the project:** This is a portability issue rather than a
direct security risk on its own — it indicates the script has only been
tested in one environment and has not been reviewed for the environment
it will actually run in (e.g., a Linux-based backend server).

